.TH "CSSCONTAINERRULE.CONDITIONS" "3JS" "June 6, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSContainerRule.conditions \- CSSContainerRule: conditions property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The read\-only \f[B]\f[CB]conditions\f[B]\f[R] property of the
\f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] interface represents an associated CSS
\f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] at\-rule as an array of objects, where each
object represents a single container condition.
.SH VALUE
An array of objects where each object has the form:
.IP
.EX
({ name: \(dq<container\-name>\(dq, query: \(dq<container\-query>\(dq });
.EE
.PP
Either the \f[CR]name\f[R] or \f[CR]query\f[R] may be the empty string,
but not both.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[B]\f[CB]conditions\f[B]\f[R] property represents an associated
CSS \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] at\-rule as an array of objects.
.PP
Each object represents a container condition as a \f[CR]name\f[R] string
property and a \f[CR]query\f[R] string property, either of which may be
the empty string if not defined.
The \f[CR]name\f[R] represents the name of a container, and the
\f[CR]query\f[R] string represents the set of feature tests that must be
true for the particular container condition to match.
.PP
For example, given the following \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]\(atcontainer\f[R] sidebar (\f[B]width\f[R] >= 700px), (\f[B]height\f[R] >= 400px) {
  \f[I]/* Styles */\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]conditions\f[R] would be an array like this:
.IP
.EX
[
  { name: \(dqsidebar\(dq, query: \(dq(width >= 700px)\(dq },
  { name: \(dq\(dq, query: \(dq(height >= 400px)\(dq },
];
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
See also Examples in \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R].
.SS Basic usage
The example shows how multiple container conditions are represented in
the \f[CR]conditions\f[R] property.
.PP
Note that we\(cqve hidden the logging code, as it is not relevant.
.SS HTML
First, we define the HTML for a \f[CR]card\f[R] contained within a
\f[CR]post\f[R].
These are represented by two nested \f[CR]<div>\f[R] elements.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqpost\(dq>
  <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqcard\(dq>
    <\f[B]h2\f[R]>Card title</\f[B]h2\f[R]>
    <\f[B]p\f[R]>Card content</\f[B]p\f[R]>
  </\f[B]div\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
The CSS for the container element specifies the type of the container,
and may also specify a name.
The card has a default font size, which is overridden when it is
contained inside a \f[CR]sidebar\f[R] \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] when its
width is greater than or equal to \f[CR]700px\f[R], or when it is inside
a container named \f[CR]other\-name\f[R].
Note that this condition is contrived to demonstrate how multiple
conditions are represented (\f[CR]other\-name\f[R] doesn\(cqt actually
do anything).
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]style\f[R] id=\(dqexample\-styles\(dq>
  .post {
    \f[B]container\-type\f[R]: inline\-size;
    \f[B]container\-name\f[R]: sidebar;
  }

  \f[I]/* Default heading styles for the card title */\f[R]
  .card h2 {
    \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 1em;
  }

  \f[B]\(atcontainer\f[R] sidebar (\f[B]width\f[R] >= 700px), other\-name {
    .card {
      \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 2em;
    }
  }
</\f[B]style\f[R]>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The code below gets the \f[CR]HTMLStyleElement\f[R] associated with the
example using its \f[CR]id\f[R], and then uses its \f[CR]sheet\f[R]
property to get the \f[CR]StyleSheet\f[R].
From the \f[CR]StyleSheet\f[R] we get the set of \f[CR]cssRules\f[R]
added to the sheet.
Since we added the \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] as the third rule above, we
can access the associated \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] using the third
entry (index \(lq2\(rq) in the \f[CR]cssRules\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] exampleStylesheet = document.getElementById(\(dqexample\-styles\(dq).sheet;
\f[B]const\f[R] exampleRules = exampleStylesheet.cssRules;
\f[B]const\f[R] containerRule = exampleRules[2]; \f[I]// a CSSContainerRule representing the container rule.\f[R]
.EE
.PP
We then use the \f[CR]containerRule\f[R] to log the value of the
\f[CR]conditions\f[R] property.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (\(dqconditions\(dq \f[B]in\f[R] CSSContainerRule.prototype) {
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions:\(dq);
  containerRule.conditions.forEach((item) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] jsonString = JSON.stringify(item);
    log(\(ga  ${jsonString}\(ga);
  });
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions is not supported.\(dq);
}
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] On browsers that don\(cqt support \f[CR]conditions\f[R],
you may be able to use \f[CR]CSSContainerRule.containerName\f[R] and
\f[CR]CSSContainerRule.containerQuery\f[R], provided that the
\f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] only specifies one container condition.
For more information, see the Feature testing example in
\f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R].
.RE
